From: Deividas Nastajus <16858119+dns54@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 03:01:14 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 12/25] UefiPayloadPkg: Add iPXE as the network driver, behind
 a setup option

EDK2 has no drivers for most NICs. The closed Realtek and ASIX UEFI
drivers, loaded from CBFS, cover only those vendors, and NETWORK_IPXE
adds iPXE as a boot application, not as a driver for EDK2's network
stack.

Add NETWORK_IPXE_DRIVER: iPXE built as a UEFI driver (ipxe.efidrv),
placed in UefiPayloadPkg/NetworkDrivers by the build. It produces the
Simple Network Protocol for every NIC iPXE supports (Realtek, Intel
I210 to I226, virtio, e1000 and more), and EDK2's own network stack
creates the UEFI PXE boot entries on top, as with a vendor driver.
Build it with EFI_DOWNGRADE_UX (iPXE's config/general.h). Otherwise
iPXE also installs its own LoadFile on each NIC, which adds a second
network boot option per NIC that boots iPXE's own stack.

With a NIC driver, every NIC gets the network stack: PXE boot options,
and the network setup pages (Network Device List, IPv4/IPv6/VLAN
configuration). Dell's firmware has them only when "Enable UEFI Network
Stack" is on, which is off by default, so a machine without an OS opens
setup instead of waiting on the network. Make the NIC driver depend on
a protocol that the boot manager installs only when the network_stack
option (coreboot CFR) is on. It installs it before connecting the
devices, whose dispatch then loads the driver.

Also install EDKII_PLATFORM_BOOT_MANAGER_PROTOCOL and drop network boot
options while the option is off, which covers a NIC with its own UEFI
option ROM.

The option is read with a new CfrHelpersLib function,
CfrOptionGetUint32(): the option's variable if set, otherwise its CFR
default.

Signed-off-by: Deividas Nastajus <16858119+dns54@users.noreply.github.com>
